[40:11] 3: Yeah, I think the getting the student athletes feedback on what their perceptions are about the use of the technology is important. I also think on the coaches and the same thing, I think, you know, if you're asking questions that the technology is going to solve, that should be something you can directly apply. I know one of the big challenges and how the world is changing is taking all this performance technology and then comparing it with the health record of the student athlete individually. So a lot of times it's, you know, is our team getting tired and is the performance there or is our performance getting better or what might have led to the risks of this rash of injuries we had going on. There has to be a way. I had education across all departments on how can we take the medical record and compare it to the data and that's going to be one of the big challenges moving forward and quite honestly where a lot of high level teams are moving because it can give you some indications on how we might be able to mitigate injury risk if we use that data correctly and then it's going to be able to get can the coaches buy into that and do they believe it? But they don't believe it until they see the numbers that you have that say, hey, here's the trends that we have seen. We need to be mindful of this. And ultimately, coaches that are super successful, unless they've just got so much more talent than everybody else, the ones that pay attention to that are probably going to get better performances from their team and their teams are going to be healthier. And so at the end of the day, that's the goal of everybody, is to be able to do that. 
