[42:10] 3: Value driven for us is really return on investment. In other words, did we, whatever we invested in, did it help us accomplish what we were trying to accomplish? And I don't think we do that nearly enough. I think we adopt something and then we just keep it and we keep utilizing it. I think Tim was right. You've got to go back and do a regular evaluation on, hey, is this technology really giving us the information that we desired and is it helping us make decisions? Again, if we're not acting on the data, we shouldn't be collecting it. And so I think for us that's really what value driven means. We made this investment. How are the staff utilizing it? How's it being used by both performance and medical side? How is it impacting our decisions? And is the value truly there for what we're spending on the technology? And I think the more we use it, the more we're getting in tune with that and the more likely you'll probably see technologies maybe be adapted and if it's not working, people will not invest in it. And so again, a lot of for me comes back to what questions are you trying to answer? And then at the end of the day, did it help you answer those? Was the value shown in the investment that you made in that technology? 
